  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What makes a name sound heroic, elvish or evil",
      "content": "<p>Readers pick up a lot from sound alone. Long names with soft consonants and endings like <em>-dil</em>, <em>-ion</em> and <em>-or</em> read as elvish and old. Short names packed with k, g, r and z read as harsh, which is why the orc names here are mostly two hard syllables (Gashgar, Kragrum). Villain names mix both: a smooth start and a sharp end, as in Thraxis or Azgaroth.</p><p>Dwarvish names here follow Old Norse patterns, ending in <em>-ulf</em>, <em>-grim</em>, <em>-rik</em> and <em>-mund</em>. Tolkien did the same thing, taking his dwarves' names from the Old Norse poem Völuspá. For recorded Viking names, see our Old Norse names list.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Giving a name more weight",
      "content": "<p>A single name can carry a character, but a second part tells readers where he comes from. Add a family or clan name (Brokdak Stonefist), a place (Saelorin of the Silver Wood) or an epithet (Morkhar the Ashen). The generators linked below can add these for you.</p><p>Try the name in a sentence your characters would actually say. \"Run, Zakinth!\" works; a name you can't shout in a hurry may be too long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Avoiding famous names",
      "content": "<p>We removed names we recognised from major series, such as the elf names printed in the Dungeons &amp; Dragons Player's Handbook and Tolkien's characters. That is a good start, not a guarantee: search any name you plan to publish, and check it doesn't mean something awkward in another language.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do you make a fantasy name?",
      "answer": "Pick the sounds your character's people would use, then combine a few syllables and a typical ending: Fael + anor, Brok + dak. Say it aloud, change one letter if it looks too like a real word, and keep the style consistent across the same culture."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are these fantasy male names unique?",
      "answer": "They are original names from our generators, and we removed ones that match well-known characters from Tolkien, Warcraft and Dungeons & Dragons. Because generators work from sound patterns, someone else may have used the same name, so search it before you publish."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do fantasy names have meanings?",
      "answer": "Not these. They are built from sounds, not words in a real language. If you want a meaning, borrow from a real language (Old Norse, Welsh, Latin) or see our lists of names that mean fire, strength or night."
    }
  ],
